$DIR/main.p:49:2: ticks is written here with no lock that guards it, while another task or an interrupt handler may use it
    ticks is written at main.p:49 in main by the main task with no lock held,
    and the interrupt handler timer installed at main.p:47 writes it at
    main.p:37 in timer. An interrupt handler may run at any point where
    interrupts are enabled, on any processor and on several at once, so these
    may run at the same time, one of them writing, which is a data race: the
    other may see a value half written, or lose an update. Volatile accesses and
    disabled interrupts exclude only the same processor.
    The compiler infers which lock guards shared data from the locks held where
    it is written: a lock that every write holds. Here no one lock is held at
    every write.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold one rt.Lock at every access, writes and reads (a handler cannot take a
    sync.Mutex):
        mu.Lock()
        ticks = v
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type from pluto/sync/atomic, such as
    atomic.Int64, and its methods at every access.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3502" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:51:10: state is read here without holding mu, which guards it
    Every write to state that another task may run at the same time holds mu:
    main.p:41 in timer (the interrupt handler timer installed at main.p:47). So
    the compiler infers that mu guards state, and every read must hold it too: a
    read without it may run while a write is half done.
    Here the main task reads it at main.p:51 in main with no lock held.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold the lock while reading:
        mu.Lock()
        v := state
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3503" for a longer explanation)
